course: dictionary
lesson: 356
title: Anteater: an animal that eats ants
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An anteater is an animal that eats ants. The word is made of two smaller words, ant and eater, and the whole word tells you what the animal does. When a new word is made of smaller words you know, look at those parts first. «Giant anteaters have no teeth; a sticky tongue about 60 centimetres long flicks up to about 150 times a minute.» The sticky tongue and the fast flicking show that the animal catches its food with its tongue.
explain: Context gives you more clues. «Giant anteaters walk on their knuckles to protect long front claws, which rip open hard termite mounds.» Termites live in mounds, so an anteater breaks into hard nests to reach them. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes the word is used more loosely. «The platypus and the echidnas (spiny anteaters of Australia and New Guinea) are mammals that lay eggs.» The brackets tell you that echidnas are called anteaters even though they are not the same animal. Brackets and commas often explain a word, so read them carefully.
fact: An anteater is a mammal with a long snout and a long sticky tongue that eats ants and termites.
fact: Anteaters live in South and Central America.
fact: «Giant anteaters» have no teeth, and they rip open termite mounds with their strong claws.
fact: Loosely, other ant-eating animals, such as the echidna and the pangolin, are sometimes called anteaters too.
exercise: «Giant anteaters have no teeth.» What do they use to rip open termite mounds? | teeth | *claws | tongue | snout
exercise: What is an anteater? | A plant that grows in hot places | A bird that sings at dawn | A large insect with wings | *An animal that eats ants
exercise: Where do anteaters live? | Antarctica | *South and Central America | Scotland | Australia
test: Which animal is loosely called an anteater, because it also eats ants? | dolphin | *echidna | lion | eagle
test: «A sticky tongue about 60 centimetres long flicks up to about 150 times a minute.» What is the anteater's tongue like? | Hard and smooth like a stone | Short and dry | Thick and heavy like a rope | *Long and sticky
exam: Which is the best meaning of anteater? | A tree with ant-shaped leaves | *A mammal with a long snout and a sticky tongue that eats ants | A bird that catches fish | A tool for digging holes
